{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310796,"nonce":"MEwOgq8ne+3gMyYAxz4kGD5f4Ro=","title":"First empirical apples-to-apples bench of rayzor vs llama.cpp on Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, M1 Pro, 80-token decode-only. rayzor 24.9 tok/s vs ...","message":"`tools/llama-diff/bench.sh` (5 runs each, 80 tokens, decode-only tok/s\nmeasured from `[done] N tokens in Ts (X tok/s)` line). Prompt: the default\nlong-form Paris-history paragraph from the bench script.\n\nSame M1 Pro, same GGUF\n(`unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-GGUF/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf`),\nsame 80-token target.\n\n\n| Backend | Median tok/s | Range | StdDev |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| rayzor (Cranelift) | **24.91** | 23.04 .. 25.07 | 0.85 |\n| llama.cpp CPU (-ngl 0) | **86.92** | 46.63 .. 96.09 | high variance |\n| llama.cpp Metal | **140.15** | 138.78 .. 141.08 | low variance |\n\nGaps:\n- rayzor → llama.cpp CPU: **3.5x**\n- rayzor → llama.cpp Metal: **5.6x**\n\n\n- \"M1 ILP saturation\" framing from perf_kernel_breakdown_full_2026_06_07.\n  llama.cpp CPU runs the same NEON SDOT kernel shape on the same silicon\n  and gets 3.5x our tok/s. M1 has substantial headroom we're not using.\n- The forecast ceilings on the 6 documented wash-outs this session\n  (Q6_K unroll, F32 GQA, SwiGLU fuse, Q4_K_M 4-block, fork-join atomic,\n  Cranelift extern intrinsics) were all in the 0.1-3% range — exactly\n  the range that doesn't matter when the real gap is 250%. The wash-outs\n  weren't wrong about M1 OoO absorbing micro-overheads; they were wrong\n  about THAT being the dominant cost question.\n\n\nThe 3.5x gap on CPU (no GPU involvement) must come from one or more of:\n\n1. **Inner SDOT throughput** — llama.cpp's `ggml_vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K` NEON\n   path might issue 2-3x more SDOT/cycle than ours. The side-by-side\n   read showed 3 algorithmic deltas but their per-super-block savings\n   summed to ~30-40 instructions — not a 3.5x lever on its own.\n2. **Threading model** — llama.cpp uses persistent spin-wait worker\n   pool dispatched ONCE per token compute graph; we dispatch\n   `parallel_rows` ~90 times per token. Per-call fork-join cost adds up.\n3. **Memory layout** — llama.cpp packs Q4_K weights into contiguous\n   slabs sized for cache prefetch; ours might be less cache-friendly.\n4. **Per-call setup** — measured at 2.7% of matmul per Investigation A,\n   so not the whole gap on its own. Could be a piece.\n5. **lm_head / output projection** — we re-quantize Q6_K → Q4_K_M at\n   load (logged in load output); llama.cpp may keep Q6_K with a\n   faster Q6_K SDOT inner.\n\nTo pin down the dominant source: run `llama-bench` (the more focused\nbenchmark tool, separate from llama-cli) with `-p 1 -n 80 -t 6` to get\nper-op timings (matmul, attention, etc) and compare bucket-by-bucket\nto the rayzor kernel_timing breakdown at HEAD. Look for the bucket\nwhere llama.cpp's wall is 2-5x less than ours.\n\nCost of that experiment: ~20 minutes. Until then, ANY proposed fix\nis speculation.\n\n\nThe 80-tok runs earlier in this session showed 32-36 tok/s. This bench\nshows 24.9 tok/s median over 5 runs. Likely causes:\n- This bench's longer prompt produces ~17 prompt tokens of prefill +\n  KV cache (decode-only number excludes prefill but includes ongoing\n  attention growth as KV cache lengthens through the 80 tokens).\n- Earlier 80-tok runs used shorter prompts (\"What is the capital of\n  France?\", \"Write a short poem...\") with less context to attend over.\n- Run-to-run variance: this bench's σ is 0.85; single-run measurements\n  can be at the high end of the distribution by luck.\n\nThe 86.9 / 24.9 = 3.5x ratio is the comparable number; both are decode-only\nmedians on the same prompt.\n\n\n- [[perf-kernel-breakdown-full-2026-06-07]] — the breakdown that\n  (wrongly) concluded \"no remaining double-digit lever\"\n- [[bugs-q4km-4block-paired-no-win]] — the microbench that suggested\n  \"M1 saturation\"\n- [[bugs-forkjoin-atomic-countdown-no-win]] — the fork-join micro-opt\n  that washed out; whose ceiling may now look different against a 3.5x\n  gap\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_llama_cpp_gap_measured` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}